 "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464053} :answer-field/id #uuid "746bc2c9-0d6d-5f8a-9494-27efcd2995b1" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cb13afc-7824-57e2-abcc-e880dfaafeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-135488" :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0 & 0.9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.9 & 0 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$  is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0.5=1 \\\\ 0.3+0.7=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c25508-a03c-54ed-af75-528b36334d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464054} {:answer/id #uuid "f997d662-f0fe-5970-be50-6cced7ae7b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f997d662-f0fe-5970-be50-6cced7ae7b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09da3cc-6896-57c1-b85f-50c6209f8fae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30af1cd-d4ae-5f09-b616-cad8cd88ec73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} {:answer/id #uuid "6256dff6-b788-5cb5-8d29-52c667534b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a30af1cd-d4ae-5f09-b616-cad8cd88ec73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c4ab03-9594-5b6b-ae8b-d04de998a6a6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50467cc1-00c3-5d22-812d-d54f3f3960c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b1cda-a69f-5c84-8c20-b1e4a0fb21d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50467cc1-00c3-5d22-812d-d54f3f3960c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3999868-4945-5e38-b4fa-18fc7ca1a6ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139455" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0174d75c-71dc-5ecb-9978-0afa7bf31836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464060} {:answer/id #uuid "1571f173-0629-58b6-bf88-d5211c7e4969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1571f173-0629-58b6-bf88-d5211c7e4969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464061} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f7c51d-3e18-5bcf-9dfe-c109b3196174" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464964} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf30537c-9bfd-5d49-9bf0-7daf53659951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464062} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9ec894-c432-504a-9be9-d97512134bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf30537c-9bfd-5d49-9bf0-7daf53659951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464062} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4369eae-3af3-5d0d-96dd-bf3c91d0e50f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464965} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30828ca0-e286-5320-a545-ecd25568f4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464064} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4b2a7-057a-56db-9141-df8234613514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4b2a7-057a-56db-9141-df8234613514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464065} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed51de2-cad2-5793-aada-609d89fdc6cf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80bdad61-ebf7-546e-ab4f-0e8486894231" :question/math-academy-id "q-139458" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef2a43dc-1c80-5218-8157-b47bfd63573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464066} {:answer/id #uuid "22e726e5-b98c-52df-9b1a-b35a9f15da31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef2a43dc-1c80-5218-8157-b47bfd63573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464066} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9e3927-b1ca-5800-a0de-54fc10194caa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6bd907c-c4b8-55fb-99be-397ec11a6b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464068} {:answer/id #uuid "ae87cd28-7716-5402-aca0-8d6623681047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6bd907c-c4b8-55fb-99be-397ec11a6b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464068} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a586de5-5ce3-5e58-8db1-7ae30b72a0c6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464968} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df519d6-28f6-5dd9-8e63-336379d28b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464070} {:answer/id #uuid "85d2b764-e3d4-5e6e-a505-4b6d357818bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df519d6-28f6-5dd9-8e63-336379d28b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464070} :answer-field/id #uuid "738f91c0-56e2-59fb-bc86-5d2fb319fe65" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a127128-cb22-56be-ba7d-5b1447ce81f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139957" :question/problem "Suppose $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n- **I.** $a\\cdot a<0$\n- **II.** $b\\cdot a>0$\n- **III.** $b\\cdot a=5$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II and III are false. Simplifying the expression $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (a+b) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $b\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aee602c-4d2a-536f-b5a1-43a412811ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464072} {:answer/id #uuid "13c7384c-1cfa-57cc-8a50-9bf3eab8a9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aee602c-4d2a-536f-b5a1-43a412811ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464072} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3b7373-e5a8-5e2b-8acc-0be10c9b59f2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "550ef764-b469-5976-ae9d-0020002e1ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464074} {:answer/id #uuid "d1697d54-6fd0-5d05-b11b-25f7ce8acc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1697d54-6fd0-5d05-b11b-25f7ce8acc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464075} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5b3e10-e84c-56fd-8992-d584ea10b2ad" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab9ce83-630e-5c19-ae07-249274247140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464076} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc5ed4c-a4d9-52b6-ad58-1348db969598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab9ce83-630e-5c19-ae07-249274247140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464076} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b13ee4f-81c8-5307-88d8-1a4665b341de" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "09aa5cfb-0ccb-582a-bfa8-b6151a1b73a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139979" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+3y=1 \\\\ 7x-2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $-3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nII. $3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8504eb0f-93c6-5d20-8195-88dbad00b315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464078} {:answer/id #uuid "c70ffc2d-dbef-5e28-8e5a-6e4a97b209b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8504eb0f-93c6-5d20-8195-88dbad00b315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464078} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c98712-e70e-5484-9096-216f3da1b3f6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464973} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855711cd-7b19-555c-ab6e-e3cbe95bee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464080} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8516b4-64ce-53f5-938e-8dfecd51f520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855711cd-7b19-555c-ab6e-e3cbe95bee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464080} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfad26a-36f9-5d0e-a1e7-36cd998ca21e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "988ccea1-2f9c-50fe-b323-1720447bfb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464082} {:answer/id #uuid "725b0b06-d3c5-567b-9157-156746df62ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725b0b06-d3c5-567b-9157-156746df62ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464083} :answer-field/id #uuid "535c6a07-6be3-5ec1-a653-291a4880438d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddc658b6-e0a7-590f-af72-c80671fc917f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139980" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y=-1 \\\\ kx+13y=-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n- **I.** $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- **II.** $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- **III.** $-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643b4a3a-7263-51ad-8b17-fdd82678cf99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e66fb8-1a18-56f4-8033-3baba4940db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643b4a3a-7263-51ad-8b17-fdd82678cf99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ceb200f-e369-571b-b619-8f16a603488c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464976} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198009c8-d9e6-556e-aa1c-228a37b12311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464086} {:answer/id #uuid "da19cf9c-0b56-5a20-b004-c70d313f5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da19cf9c-0b56-5a20-b004-c70d313f5184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98905ac-4493-5551-96b2-6d52f8e3f566" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ea76cf-d936-573e-a0be-56de7954b8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464088} {:answer/id #uuid "24e2aa19-faca-5a81-a238-e9e984e89ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24e2aa19-faca-5a81-a238-e9e984e89ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464089} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed42e6d2-1aff-5708-ad8c-b45de0195450" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ does not have identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ does not have proportional columns\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dcaf5cb-763f-5cf3-9460-94fc22fd63a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464090} {:answer/id #uuid "94b51605-0792-5dfb-9caa-c9f83344d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b51605-0792-5dfb-9caa-c9f83344d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464091} :answer-field/id #uuid "977b3534-4011-5dfd-a7b9-79927cd06b86" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc8b5695-d93e-519e-8d9e-dc6c8a41f9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464092} {:answer/id #uuid "6b733140-0c4b-5274-8eb8-dcc1ee96785c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8b5695-d93e-519e-8d9e-dc6c8a41f9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464092} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5c0b84-632c-50f3-8754-70310b21a57f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f726adc9-d1dc-521c-8654-53c52267101e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464094} {:answer/id #uuid "94789da1-7829-587f-bfab-9c55f7f2211d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f726adc9-d1dc-521c-8654-53c52267101e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464094} :answer-field/id #uuid "121070ea-b2d0-524b-8159-ea2fbac7bd33" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14e63fd3-62b7-575e-b489-4cda5c20ed53" :question/math-academy-id "q-140050" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has proportional columns\n- **II.** $A$ has identical columns\n- **III.** $A$ has identical rows\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afa82d44-d03d-5e9b-af04-272ab90230e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464096} {:answer/id #uuid "71f897c8-dfce-5b45-8f4c-bb7062ae467e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa82d44-d03d-5e9b-af04-272ab90230e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464096} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed222a1-7190-5964-99bc-a9de5fd0be28" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a1a3dca-d308-5f38-927f-67b0c977e32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464098} {:answer/id #uuid "d292b59f-adc1-58d1-98bc-5da647707c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d292b59f-adc1-58d1-98bc-5da647707c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464099} :answer-field/id #uuid "cafebd75-e7fa-54e1-9bcf-2578d3061be4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb177f8-879b-5898-9551-53329abe91c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464100} {:answer/id #uuid "7332d203-8792-51a5-976c-10e58a391aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7332d203-8792-51a5-976c-10e58a391aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464101} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8de8dc2-3957-5b43-9b1f-bfdfb462141b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cac76f2-abeb-58d4-87e9-5f3619bedd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140085" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n- **II.** $T$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "497c7760-d42f-5692-9a2b-f696bcb0055b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464102} {:answer/id #uuid "277a5a87-418d-51a1-a726-67cf72fc412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "277a5a87-418d-51a1-a726-67cf72fc412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464103} :answer-field/id #uuid "83086f7a-b7cb-5812-935f-60e558109693" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8236707-38fd-5c0b-89a7-0a27fffeb00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464104} {:answer/id #uuid "57b74907-7a7f-5031-9194-c6a7d4bdc948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8236707-38fd-5c0b-89a7-0a27fffeb00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464104} :answer-field/id #uuid "274eab40-126e-5394-8c41-1828340662f4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8913ca1a-f4ac-570e-9fa4-f8c757505f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464106} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc3a2fc-28db-525b-8a74-baf7b5fc3108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fc3a2fc-28db-525b-8a74-baf7b5fc3108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464107} :answer-field/id #uuid "080e9bc1-5374-5406-97b7-4e6bef226207" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0e37cf1-8e7e-52da-9921-f2cfa10fc56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140434" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}$ and $g(t)=3{t}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u={t}^{3}+t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a096c2-c6c3-52ba-8ac3-585757485178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464108} {:answer/id #uuid "56ad38ea-3da9-54a5-b599-12a437f08a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56ad38ea-3da9-54a5-b599-12a437f08a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464109} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd73ff08-a0f2-55a4-ab61-bf0d8769c609" :answer-field/key "U" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c0fff84-54a3-54cd-a2e6-fe2346e5c3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464110} {:answer/id #uuid "82a0f2d6-342e-528e-be81-c9d0b902af44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0fff84-54a3-54cd-a2e6-fe2346e5c3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464110} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3a8733-5fd0-54bc-836c-e7cc8f83b523" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c76788ef-a5ec-5512-9168-7ccf38baeca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464112} {:answer/id #uuid "5633e640-236c-5992-b88d-01b8d795c3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5633e640-236c-5992-b88d-01b8d795c3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464113} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2030c1-66d7-59ae-9ef7-a05da9bdf30c" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b412ef2-b86f-5688-ad69-1945a2870af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-140480" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n- **U.** $U={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **V.** $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **W.** $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0+3-3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0-3+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe7b5c5f-a26d-5d34-bfcb-d5696b3c7942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464114} {:answer/id #uuid "1bab4da3-03b0-5c35-95f9-e1837ab424f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bab4da3-03b0-5c35-95f9-e1837ab424f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464115} :answer-field/id #uuid "f11ee610-452b-5628-a0a2-72ae40e7ab77" :answer-field/key "U" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1366dbcb-9dad-5292-ba82-6f19179612a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464116} {:answer/id #uuid "d3421cd8-7243-549f-baa8-2099100e2b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3421cd8-7243-549f-baa8-2099100e2b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464117} :answer-field/id #uuid "3853292a-fe0b-516d-9a99-df4ebadfedb6" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddafd2b2-18ea-5850-b12e-4909c1360753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464118} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b80115-a2f6-5830-9801-70d1e6f5eed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddafd2b2-18ea-5850-b12e-4909c1360753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464118} :answer-field/id #uuid "b01ebad9-f142-5b89-860c-309aff28bda8" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d09c8cc-56af-54e4-b26f-33ef3a4b7c45" :question/math-academy-id "q-140483" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **V.** $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **W.** $W={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}+0-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ee4807-0768-5dc5-aeb6-4da0e5622499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464120} {:answer/id #uuid "867eac9d-8432-5a6b-a7e2-86a32542c8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867eac9d-8432-5a6b-a7e2-86a32542c8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464121} :answer-field/id #uuid "38be1c21-f6e9-54d4-9142-3c32d41421a3" :answer-field/key "U" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b479a3-7665-5809-93c8-9923bd9dd078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464122} {:answer/id #uuid "0434d7ec-2c3f-5911-b5ee-e68fee2943f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11b479a3-7665-5809-93c8-9923bd9dd078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464122} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7fe6036-bc87-5e27-87c6-c994de7b29fc" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6e7f0ef-5080-5bcb-a31e-245444bb3767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464124} {:answer/id #uuid "8a380d84-b266-5704-bc8b-eadf0657fcbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e7f0ef-5080-5bcb-a31e-245444bb3767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464124} :answer-field/id #uuid "988bc0b9-cec8-5860-8c76-531f6f5c225e" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "762f53a4-6eba-533a-aefb-847dcb22ecb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140484" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **V.** $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **W.** $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6}{13}-\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=4+9=13\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14825df6-62a0-58c7-84f8-874b33d80648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464126} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fbbaf0-77de-5023-a066-2a3d3242c32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14825df6-62a0-58c7-84f8-874b33d80648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464126} :answer-field/id #uuid "5210290a-0c21-5691-8070-7eab6efd06ea" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464997} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25fed85-ab3b-5a31-8f1d-2a199858b504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464128} {:answer/id #uuid "5b56749b-79d6-59cb-8f18-a65746c114a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25fed85-ab3b-5a31-8f1d-2a199858b504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464128} :answer-field/id #uuid "09f16e55-e049-5129-afae-8b306d36faba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a92bd8e-cc40-5cc1-818c-7a58e0faa065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464130} {:answer/id #uuid "71370a5b-7609-5a96-aecd-d3b1db769e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a92bd8e-cc40-5cc1-818c-7a58e0faa065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464130} :answer-field/id #uuid "0148a50b-ed82-5196-9619-6267975e2539" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0aa2e7ba-284c-52fb-a0ea-9a38be5541e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140500" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-{e}^{t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(e+{e}^{-1})-(e-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-{e}^{-t}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-1}-e)-({e}^{-1}-e) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98311a5d-c789-50b1-998c-82789cf3e15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464132} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1caad2-1552-53b2-ad65-0faf937f9cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a1caad2-1552-53b2-ad65-0faf937f9cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464133} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d3e7a6-f070-518f-bca6-72c0a2e523f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2950a552-7b64-54f3-9df5-37fbc1daf708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464134} {:answer/id #uuid "57bc0bcf-2d74-5b42-80b8-a9462aac80d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2950a552-7b64-54f3-9df5-37fbc1daf708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464134} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e24475-4728-5731-901b-4204e0ce6cef" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427e5f77-75e3-5ea5-8f93-8f5a66733617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464136} {:answer/id #uuid "01222f75-c4bd-542d-8467-b02dd4c8ea9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427e5f77-75e3-5ea5-8f93-8f5a66733617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464136} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74724c4-d89e-52f6-b857-07eecbd822cb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3d61daa-c943-5ecf-a98c-7f9bcd210491" :question/math-academy-id "q-140565" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n- **I.** If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- **II.** If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** $Q-I$ is singular\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4ff535-a1f0-5ce6-8b0f-0685c923aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464138} {:answer/id #uuid "e9853f79-3460-5bbc-b09c-1486f81f69b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d4ff535-a1f0-5ce6-8b0f-0685c923aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464138} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e19efd-b193-5d8c-951e-06d2594d3c69" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d591cdfe-787c-561e-b1ab-96a237381b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464140} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd54853-c89a-5086-aedb-4b0387aa7aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bd54853-c89a-5086-aedb-4b0387aa7aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464141} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d9f0ad-5dbc-52a9-a14d-c0423c3659b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d063335-7f55-523f-94d2-bc87bd49b8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464142} {:answer/id #uuid "c08d56be-7940-5e02-8c76-d6c91fd825db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d063335-7f55-523f-94d2-bc87bd49b8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464142} :answer-field/id #uuid "708f3344-420a-5f82-a998-358eeabd9c9b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27c4d64e-3a6c-52f2-bb70-03acd0cf6bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140834" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- **II.** $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- **III.** $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355ee90c-29a8-5f7d-9858-d5d774afc427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464144} {:answer/id #uuid "52c13329-ed29-5a6a-9c3a-980f9b642801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355ee90c-29a8-5f7d-9858-d5d774afc427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464144} :answer-field/id #uuid "57268d9e-f878-521e-88c1-e38ad05c4b25" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fca2a6a-5f0e-57c2-b88a-83793aa0ea03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464146} {:answer/id #uuid "d9140ca1-8724-5815-b59d-81a85f001cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fca2a6a-5f0e-57c2-b88a-83793aa0ea03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464146} :answer-field/id #uuid "d672360c-79a2-53e5-89fd-18eb4ebb73b0" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "729a3d56-7c5c-50c8-b093-a5ba7c6c0128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464148} {:answer/id #uuid "a192252a-3f86-5dc5-852c-3bafb620daf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a192252a-3f86-5dc5-852c-3bafb620daf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464149} :answer-field/id #uuid "045f8e77-8eca-5ea2-86b3-e877ff4aa8cc" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d68a509c-d790-5483-ab3b-63aef29c3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140870" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce697823-b9ba-531c-b6f6-70c01e71b761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464150} {:answer/id #uuid "80fb9aca-744f-5fe8-afe1-cdac1ac5b86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80fb9aca-744f-5fe8-afe1-cdac1ac5b86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464151} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ae853f-8dce-5116-80a7-2c8ac7eba9c7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5c4e5a-3e5f-5204-b749-a5e0440b79b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464152} {:answer/id #uuid "e233e265-68ee-5d13-a722-52a821c352f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5c4e5a-3e5f-5204-b749-a5e0440b79b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464152} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3ea246-aa5b-5727-95be-be543e633a91" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4ef20b-9de9-53a0-84f4-1b5455e1dafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464154} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9e1cf6-558c-558b-b78c-b49fab9a0c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a4ef20b-9de9-53a0-84f4-1b5455e1dafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464154} :answer-field/id #uuid "c96e675e-3b15-5e60-9aa2-5c5f606831d2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7059aa8-79d3-5da6-b8bc-f64a765f9c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141014" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x<0 \\\\ -1, & x=0 \\\\ x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **I.** $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- **II.** $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- **III.** $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\frac{1}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a46dca9-6a77-532b-a621-e5e80789d8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464156} {:answer/id #uuid "5799ce27-8068-5b2f-8fc8-1f78767a9193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a46dca9-6a77-532b-a621-e5e80789d8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464156} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b70a5b-de1a-57c7-b6ce-d41bca7868cc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029a3a0e-cfc6-552a-aa69-8f2a2cb429c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464158} {:answer/id #uuid "518c434f-e88b-53ed-afdc-7cca270b0fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029a3a0e-cfc6-552a-aa69-8f2a2cb429c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464158} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f08f511-e51f-5132-9f31-250abcbd76a9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07badc24-5a5f-5098-9c7b-2981336dbdc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464160} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0605c1-7e2a-5150-b68c-ac31d743ba94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07badc24-5a5f-5098-9c7b-2981336dbdc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464160} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54d83fd-9fa4-552f-9d17-d870629c26c9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a7b393e-0e8e-512c-933e-c05c7c1e88c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141016" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x<\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 1, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 2x, & x>\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **I.** $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- **II.** $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- **III.** $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}h(x) & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne h(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "988e81c4-45bb-54db-b4b2-1ec2c36ff182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464162} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce495f2-062e-58e7-a0de-68a83e12a6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988e81c4-45bb-54db-b4b2-1ec2c36ff182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464162} :answer-field/id #uuid "883267ec-1fe0-50b6-86d2-7b796edd3287" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65570c34-7b80-5bbf-8f7b-e95de7fe096f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464164} {:answer/id #uuid "725aa7c0-9778-5c52-b7e7-eb03ec0ecd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725aa7c0-9778-5c52-b7e7-eb03ec0ecd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464165} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e9a163-3bd7-5759-a715-0e15d8d082ae" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61bffde0-2eb4-57d2-8617-cdd9d92f31d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464166} {:answer/id #uuid "35c2be1c-5675-5964-8242-63f9c8659250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61bffde0-2eb4-57d2-8617-cdd9d92f31d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464166} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7af2d1-d887-5751-9373-e70956735d2d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3376f0ce-d656-5952-8ec5-482d4a788f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-141017" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x<-1 \\\\ 1, & x=-1 \\\\ x+2, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **I.** $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- **II.** $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- **III.** $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}h(x) & =2(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-2+1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}h(x) & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec25885-aa40-5fcb-b44c-0a2e688c0506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464168} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c96a74-965a-517e-b5f6-459f984a277d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ec25885-aa40-5fcb-b44c-0a2e688c0506" :answer/type {:db/i