tion.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a9082ef-b5c5-508a-ad30-c16427769a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141209" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals have finite bounds of integration and an integrand that is unbounded at an endpoint or an interior point of the interval (improper integrals of the second kind on a finite interval)?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}-8}\\,$\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216f387e-b811-56e8-a456-90caf42a0c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464174} {:answer/id #uuid "dc902c84-eab1-5a4d-9091-d15db1897217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "216f387e-b811-56e8-a456-90caf42a0c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464174} :answer-field/id 