ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcc3f3f7-a58b-5346-bd11-154e7ec00b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464570} {:answer/id #uuid "f3631fc6-16fb-54ad-b7a6-67dc5833b7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uu