/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bac4e6a1-90fc-5ecd-80e4-898747c5b764" :question/math-academy-id "q-158424" :question/problem "![](images/81/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b454dc07-632b-5740-8c95-b8159f47d80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464576} {:answer/id #uuid "83dd29aa-b10f-5ea6-aaa0-9799e